WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.417 --> 00:00:03.027
- Hi everyone, Sal Khan
here from Khan Academy.

00:00:03.027 --> 00:00:04.747
Welcome to our Homeroom live stream.

00:00:04.747 --> 00:00:06.247
Very excited about today's guest,

00:00:06.247 --> 00:00:07.927
Superintendent Austin Beutner

00:00:07.927 --> 00:00:10.997
from Los Angeles Unified School District.

00:00:10.997 --> 00:00:13.177
So already, start thinking
about some questions

00:00:13.177 --> 00:00:14.957
you might have for him and put

00:00:14.957 --> 00:00:16.357
them in the message boards on Facebook

00:00:16.357 --> 00:00:18.647
and YouTube or below this
video, or next to this video,

00:00:18.647 --> 00:00:20.537
wherever it shows up and we'll try to get

00:00:20.537 --> 00:00:22.037
to as many of those questions as we can.

00:00:22.037 --> 00:00:24.047
But before we start
into that conversation,

00:00:24.047 --> 00:00:26.267
I will give my standard reminders.

00:00:26.267 --> 00:00:27.100
First of all,

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.257
that Khan Academy is not
for profit organization.

00:00:29.257 --> 00:00:31.007
We can only exist with
philanthropic donations

00:00:31.007 --> 00:00:32.820
from folks like yourself.
So if you're in a position

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.657
to do so, please go to
khanacademy.org/donate.

00:00:35.657 --> 00:00:38.727
Also a special shout out
to several organizations

00:00:38.727 --> 00:00:42.047
that have helped Khan
Academy really accelerate

00:00:42.047 --> 00:00:44.157
what we're trying to do
during the COVID crisis.

00:00:44.157 --> 00:00:44.990
As you can imagine,

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:47.107
a lot of folks are leaning
on us more than ever,

00:00:47.107 --> 00:00:48.807
that's made our server costs go up,

00:00:48.807 --> 00:00:50.307
we're trying to accelerate programs.

00:00:50.307 --> 00:00:53.467
So special thanks to Bank of America,

00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:54.977
Google.org,

00:00:54.977 --> 00:00:56.607
AT&amp;T, Fastly,

00:00:56.607 --> 00:01:00.007
Novartis, and the many other
supporters of Khan Academy

00:01:00.007 --> 00:01:01.787
over the years to allow
us what we're doing,

00:01:01.787 --> 00:01:04.147
but we're still running into
dust, so we need your help.

00:01:04.147 --> 00:01:08.087
Also one last announcement,
just reminder of our podcast.

00:01:08.087 --> 00:01:08.920
So where we take

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:12.207
the interesting bits of
our conversations here

00:01:12.207 --> 00:01:14.967
and I suspect today is gonna
be one of them and we put

00:01:14.967 --> 00:01:17.567
into a podcast so that
you can actually listen

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:20.637
to the Homeroom while you
drive or do other things

00:01:20.637 --> 00:01:23.367
without creating some
type of a safety hazard.

00:01:23.367 --> 00:01:25.907
So definitely check out
Homeroom with Sal, the podcast,

00:01:25.907 --> 00:01:29.017
wherever you find your podcasts.

00:01:29.017 --> 00:01:31.587
So with that, I'm excited to introduce

00:01:31.587 --> 00:01:33.877
Superintendent Austin Beutner.

00:01:33.877 --> 00:01:36.437
Superintendent Beutner,
thanks for joining.

00:01:36.437 --> 00:01:37.277
- Sal, thanks for having me.

00:01:37.277 --> 00:01:38.827
I really appreciate you taking the time

00:01:38.827 --> 00:01:41.477
to have this conversation.

00:01:41.477 --> 00:01:44.187
- So maybe a good place to start
is just gonna lay the land.

00:01:44.187 --> 00:01:47.807
You are in charge of the
second largest school district

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:51.857
in the country, highly complex
job under any circumstances,

00:01:51.857 --> 00:01:53.587
but obviously under the last six

00:01:53.587 --> 00:01:56.227
or seven months have
been especially unusual.

00:01:56.227 --> 00:01:57.777
I just love kind of a background

00:01:57.777 --> 00:01:59.547
of how have you handled this?

00:01:59.547 --> 00:02:02.027
Like, what was it like last March, April,

00:02:02.027 --> 00:02:03.777
as you were just, as the school closures

00:02:03.777 --> 00:02:04.867
were starting to happen?

00:02:04.867 --> 00:02:06.177
How have you navigated through the summer

00:02:06.177 --> 00:02:08.047
and how are you thinking about it now?

00:02:08.047 --> 00:02:08.880
- Great.

00:02:11.627 --> 00:02:12.907
We're in the midst of a crisis

00:02:12.907 --> 00:02:14.407
that none of us have ever seen before.

00:02:14.407 --> 00:02:17.917
But if we remember where our North star,

00:02:17.917 --> 00:02:19.607
the constellation North stars for us are,

00:02:19.607 --> 00:02:24.607
which is student learning,
support for the families we serve

00:02:24.637 --> 00:02:26.787
and making sure we take
care of our employees

00:02:26.787 --> 00:02:29.937
who do those first two
things, we're gonna get there.

00:02:29.937 --> 00:02:30.797
Now, as you mentioned,

00:02:30.797 --> 00:02:32.417
Los Angeles is kind of big and unruly.

00:02:32.417 --> 00:02:34.387
We have about 700,000 students,

00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:37.247
more than 80% of them are from
families living in poverty,

00:02:37.247 --> 00:02:40.597
75,000 employees spread
across 700 square miles.

00:02:40.597 --> 00:02:43.307
So, much of what we need to
do we need to do in scale.

00:02:43.307 --> 00:02:44.987
We need to be able to take an idea

00:02:44.987 --> 00:02:47.620
and turn it into a reality and scale.

00:02:48.547 --> 00:02:50.997
And what we've tried to do is respond

00:02:50.997 --> 00:02:53.167
to those same set of priorities.

00:02:53.167 --> 00:02:57.277
We immediately converted our school lunch

00:02:57.277 --> 00:02:59.847
effort into a community
food relief effort.

00:02:59.847 --> 00:03:02.357
We've now provided more
than 65 million meals

00:03:02.357 --> 00:03:04.027
to hungry children and adults.

00:03:04.027 --> 00:03:07.837
We're the largest school
relief effort in the country.

00:03:07.837 --> 00:03:10.607
We've provided almost 10 million items

00:03:10.607 --> 00:03:12.387
with that, whether they are books,

00:03:12.387 --> 00:03:13.807
noise canceling headphones,

00:03:13.807 --> 00:03:16.777
recreational sports materials,

00:03:16.777 --> 00:03:19.340
baby supplies, with that, alongside that.

00:03:20.327 --> 00:03:22.707
We made the effort to make
sure we could keep students

00:03:22.707 --> 00:03:25.877
connected like this with
their school community,

00:03:25.877 --> 00:03:27.887
so we bought computers.

00:03:27.887 --> 00:03:30.497
We worked in March alongside
Apple to pull inventory

00:03:30.497 --> 00:03:31.330
out of their stores to make sure

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:34.050
that we could get our
youngest learners iPads.

00:03:34.987 --> 00:03:37.507
We worked with Verizon
to create a template

00:03:38.587 --> 00:03:40.627
that we could buy very cheaply

00:03:40.627 --> 00:03:43.517
to dramatically reduce
price for internet access

00:03:43.517 --> 00:03:44.604
that we could provide to students

00:03:44.604 --> 00:03:46.140
and their families for free.

00:03:47.267 --> 00:03:49.137
For our students, many of
them live in the wrong side

00:03:49.137 --> 00:03:50.407
of escaping digital divide.

00:03:50.407 --> 00:03:51.947
We tend to think of it as that area

00:03:51.947 --> 00:03:53.937
between South and North Dakota,

00:03:53.937 --> 00:03:55.177
where there aren't cell towers,

00:03:55.177 --> 00:03:56.597
but actually in many of
the committees we serve

00:03:56.597 --> 00:03:57.687
families can't afford broadband.

00:03:57.687 --> 00:04:01.667
So we set out to do that as
cost-effectively, as we could.

00:04:01.667 --> 00:04:04.317
We set about training our
educators in what it means

00:04:04.317 --> 00:04:06.367
to teach and engage online

00:04:06.367 --> 00:04:07.567
and we've had three rounds of that.

00:04:07.567 --> 00:04:11.507
We took all 35,000 through
a basic set of trainings.

00:04:11.507 --> 00:04:15.847
We allowed another 15,000
to go for what I'll call

00:04:15.847 --> 00:04:17.027
the masters in it and then we did

00:04:17.027 --> 00:04:18.827
it again during the summer.

00:04:18.827 --> 00:04:21.997
We did that all without missing
a day of school by the way.

00:04:21.997 --> 00:04:23.987
To respond to learning
needs in the summer,

00:04:23.987 --> 00:04:26.117
for the first time ever, we offered

00:04:26.117 --> 00:04:27.427
summer school for every student

00:04:27.427 --> 00:04:29.457
in our school district, everyone.

00:04:29.457 --> 00:04:31.157
And we tried to take
some creative approaches

00:04:31.157 --> 00:04:32.627
and you and I had this conversation before

00:04:32.627 --> 00:04:34.737
about what student engagement
actually looks like

00:04:34.737 --> 00:04:37.197
in this form of teaching and learning,

00:04:37.197 --> 00:04:38.717
so we brought in
different set of partners.

00:04:38.717 --> 00:04:40.787
James Cameron took some of
our high school students

00:04:40.787 --> 00:04:42.897
on a voyager to the Titanic,
reading and literacy,

00:04:42.897 --> 00:04:45.657
math and absolute engagements.

00:04:45.657 --> 00:04:49.907
With Fender music, we
created virtual music classes

00:04:49.907 --> 00:04:51.837
where Fender provided free guitars.

00:04:51.837 --> 00:04:54.027
We had a thousand people
participate in the summer,

00:04:54.027 --> 00:04:56.887
we kept it going to 2,000 more this fall.

00:04:56.887 --> 00:04:59.897
The creators of "Despicable
Me" and "Secret Life of Pets"

00:04:59.897 --> 00:05:01.427
worked with us on animation

00:05:01.427 --> 00:05:04.237
and so students learned
to draw and tell stories.

00:05:04.237 --> 00:05:06.407
And with Snapchat we created a book club.

00:05:06.407 --> 00:05:08.487
And as much as I might
like recommend books

00:05:08.487 --> 00:05:11.697
to our high schoolers,
Alicia Keys over Snapchat,

00:05:11.697 --> 00:05:13.717
we reach students where they are.

00:05:13.717 --> 00:05:14.797
And it was pretty exciting

00:05:14.797 --> 00:05:16.007
to see the day we launched that.

00:05:16.007 --> 00:05:17.977
Within moments, 500 of our students

00:05:17.977 --> 00:05:20.607
had responded to her storytelling

00:05:20.607 --> 00:05:23.197
about a book that she's
reading or had read,

00:05:23.197 --> 00:05:24.627
and they could download it for free

00:05:24.627 --> 00:05:27.247
out of some charitable funds we raised,

00:05:27.247 --> 00:05:29.037
500 students in the
first just few minutes.

00:05:29.037 --> 00:05:30.937
So we've tried to do things differently.

00:05:30.937 --> 00:05:33.187
And I think I was a little
bit late, I apologize,

00:05:33.187 --> 00:05:34.587
I was just coming from
taking a COVID test.

00:05:34.587 --> 00:05:38.387
The next chapter for us is
we've set up a system to provide

00:05:38.387 --> 00:05:41.017
testing for the COVID virus
and all the contact tracing

00:05:41.017 --> 00:05:42.057
that go with us in schools,

00:05:42.057 --> 00:05:45.367
because we think it has to
be part of a safe return

00:05:45.367 --> 00:05:47.357
for all of the school
community to campuses.

00:05:47.357 --> 00:05:49.647
So we have to do things different,

00:05:49.647 --> 00:05:51.237
is part of the nature of it.

00:05:51.237 --> 00:05:52.367
One of the things I hope we do talk

00:05:52.367 --> 00:05:53.717
about is how we're learning

00:05:53.717 --> 00:05:55.917
from this and how some
of what we're learning

00:05:55.917 --> 00:05:58.437
will make us in the system better I think

00:05:58.437 --> 00:05:59.677
when we get back to campuses,

00:05:59.677 --> 00:06:02.467
but there's no substitute
for being in schools.

00:06:02.467 --> 00:06:03.300
- Absolutely.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:04.133
And maybe, you know,

00:06:04.133 --> 00:06:05.837
there's so much of what
you just talked about.

00:06:05.837 --> 00:06:07.387
Some very cool projects, I guess

00:06:07.387 --> 00:06:11.487
reminding us that Los Angeles
is a fairly unique place.

00:06:11.487 --> 00:06:14.707
But some of what you
mentioned, the lunch programs,

00:06:14.707 --> 00:06:17.407
especially trying to
close the digital divide

00:06:17.407 --> 00:06:20.967
or at least make it not
as much of an issue.

00:06:20.967 --> 00:06:23.237
I'm curious, you know, we've been hearing,

00:06:23.237 --> 00:06:24.977
talking to superintendents,
district officials

00:06:24.977 --> 00:06:27.787
around the country that one,
I mean, that's not easy.

00:06:27.787 --> 00:06:30.227
I am curious how you are
able to get the devices out.

00:06:30.227 --> 00:06:32.657
I've read other places
outside of our conversations,

00:06:32.657 --> 00:06:33.490
many tens of thousands

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:36.727
or even hundreds of thousands
of devices y'all pulled off.

00:06:36.727 --> 00:06:38.927
But are you finding
that there's still kids

00:06:38.927 --> 00:06:41.387
that are disengaged even after you get

00:06:41.387 --> 00:06:42.487
the devices out there?

00:06:44.307 --> 00:06:47.597
- Not easy to get devices,
so we looked ahead.

00:06:47.597 --> 00:06:49.547
The old observation (indistinct)

00:06:49.547 --> 00:06:51.147
when you see a toddler walk,

00:06:51.147 --> 00:06:53.827
they bump into things 'cause
they're looking at their feet.

00:06:53.827 --> 00:06:54.997
You need to look down
the road a little bit.

00:06:54.997 --> 00:06:56.697
So we looked down the road and said, okay,

00:06:56.697 --> 00:06:58.797
in February we started ordering devices.

00:06:58.797 --> 00:07:00.927
We said, this could go this way,

00:07:00.927 --> 00:07:03.397
they won't go bad, we'll figure it out.

00:07:03.397 --> 00:07:04.267
In March--

00:07:04.267 --> 00:07:06.947
- In February, that's actually worth,

00:07:06.947 --> 00:07:08.357
I didn't remember anyone really talking

00:07:08.357 --> 00:07:09.457
about school closures in February.

00:07:09.457 --> 00:07:11.407
So y'all were thinking
of, y'all were doing

00:07:11.407 --> 00:07:13.137
some (indistinct)
contingency planning, okay.

00:07:13.137 --> 00:07:15.107
- Yeah, yeah, we're gonna look at that.

00:07:15.107 --> 00:07:16.917
Now engagement takes many different forms.

00:07:16.917 --> 00:07:20.057
So the first measure we have
is every student connected.

00:07:20.057 --> 00:07:21.157
When we started in March,

00:07:21.157 --> 00:07:24.727
we were short about 15 or 18% of the kids.

00:07:24.727 --> 00:07:26.897
Within about four weeks 100% connected

00:07:26.897 --> 00:07:28.507
'cause we said first,

00:07:28.507 --> 00:07:29.557
they have to be part of
the school community.

00:07:29.557 --> 00:07:32.537
Then we started to measure
what does connected mean?

00:07:32.537 --> 00:07:34.437
Are they engaging in classes

00:07:34.437 --> 00:07:37.807
through all of our
different tools and devices?

00:07:37.807 --> 00:07:40.197
- How many devices did you
have to get out to do that?

00:07:40.197 --> 00:07:41.837
I mean, just logistically,
that seems hard.

00:07:41.837 --> 00:07:43.807
15% of 700,000 kids,

00:07:43.807 --> 00:07:45.309
you're looking at over 100, 000 devices.

00:07:45.309 --> 00:07:46.267
- More than that.

00:07:46.267 --> 00:07:48.387
15% who when we

00:07:51.636 --> 00:07:53.207
hadn't connected, but it's
actually more than that.

00:07:53.207 --> 00:07:55.257
So it was hundreds of thousands of devices

00:07:55.257 --> 00:07:57.907
and hundreds of thousands
of internet connections,

00:07:57.907 --> 00:08:00.867
which is why again, we had to
reach out to Verizon and say,

00:08:00.867 --> 00:08:02.037
can we do something different here?

00:08:02.037 --> 00:08:05.547
Because the bespoke one by one connecting

00:08:05.547 --> 00:08:07.517
wasn't gonna work, we
needed a wholesale system

00:08:07.517 --> 00:08:09.307
to quickly get to that point.

00:08:09.307 --> 00:08:11.707
And then we started
talking to our partners,

00:08:11.707 --> 00:08:14.087
Khan Academy, amongst them to say,

00:08:14.087 --> 00:08:15.877
how do we teach online?

00:08:15.877 --> 00:08:17.557
What materials can we supplement with?

00:08:17.557 --> 00:08:19.125
So whether it's Zoom or Nearpod

00:08:19.125 --> 00:08:22.397
as connecting or communication technology

00:08:22.397 --> 00:08:26.117
or different apps that may
be math or language focused,

00:08:26.117 --> 00:08:28.707
we reached out to all of
them and said, we need more.

00:08:28.707 --> 00:08:30.797
We need more from you,
we need to learn more,

00:08:30.797 --> 00:08:31.907
we need to measure engagement,

00:08:31.907 --> 00:08:33.947
we need for with the efficacy or usage.

00:08:33.947 --> 00:08:36.837
Use case is for all of
these because for us,

00:08:36.837 --> 00:08:38.757
technology has always been a supplement,

00:08:38.757 --> 00:08:40.757
it was never the main event.

00:08:40.757 --> 00:08:41.987
We took a quick inventory.

00:08:41.987 --> 00:08:44.047
When our teachers were
going through the trainings

00:08:44.047 --> 00:08:45.657
we were able to survey.

00:08:45.657 --> 00:08:48.387
There were more than 400
different tools and technologies

00:08:48.387 --> 00:08:50.427
and use in our classrooms,
that's too many.

00:08:50.427 --> 00:08:52.107
As a supplement, great.

00:08:52.107 --> 00:08:53.567
What is the core and the focus,

00:08:53.567 --> 00:08:55.657
we had to figure out
where to place our bets,

00:08:55.657 --> 00:08:57.517
which ones were most widely used,

00:08:57.517 --> 00:08:59.657
what our feedback from our educators are.

00:08:59.657 --> 00:09:02.467
And we continue to have working
groups where our teachers

00:09:02.467 --> 00:09:05.447
are working alongside
the technology providers

00:09:05.447 --> 00:09:08.327
to give them feedback,
so as the next generation

00:09:08.327 --> 00:09:10.147
or the next need we have it's built

00:09:10.147 --> 00:09:12.777
into the service they can provide for us.

00:09:12.777 --> 00:09:15.157
So we measure connection,

00:09:15.157 --> 00:09:16.957
we measure time utilization,

00:09:16.957 --> 00:09:18.387
we measure assignments.

00:09:18.387 --> 00:09:21.677
And one of the things that you
and I spoke when we first met

00:09:21.677 --> 00:09:25.087
a long time ago now, how
you're able to tell us

00:09:25.087 --> 00:09:28.137
just how much engagement
there was on your platform,

00:09:28.137 --> 00:09:30.597
in the various parts of
it amongst our teachers.

00:09:30.597 --> 00:09:31.807
Well, that's very useful for us

00:09:31.807 --> 00:09:33.827
because we know there's a use case.

00:09:33.827 --> 00:09:35.227
The customer's told us something

00:09:35.227 --> 00:09:38.057
and we have a lot more available

00:09:38.057 --> 00:09:41.447
to us in terms of a
digital footprint today,

00:09:41.447 --> 00:09:43.397
how we use that wisely,

00:09:43.397 --> 00:09:47.247
what the shortcomings are, how
that does or doesn't compare

00:09:47.247 --> 00:09:49.817
to a classroom setting.

00:09:49.817 --> 00:09:50.717
You know, those are questions,

00:09:50.717 --> 00:09:52.027
we're still impacting some of them.

00:09:52.027 --> 00:09:54.507
I mean, people say,
what's your attendance?

00:09:54.507 --> 00:09:57.577
Well, we can measure physical attendance.

00:09:57.577 --> 00:09:58.777
Okay, now we assume a lot of things.

00:09:58.777 --> 00:10:00.597
We assume Sal's in a seat in the classroom

00:10:00.597 --> 00:10:01.727
and therefore he must be paying attention

00:10:01.727 --> 00:10:02.717
or learning something.

00:10:02.717 --> 00:10:04.107
Well, that's not always the case.

00:10:04.107 --> 00:10:07.457
The converse may be true
but Sal has not logged on.

00:10:07.457 --> 00:10:08.547
Well, he might be reading a book,

00:10:08.547 --> 00:10:10.827
might be working on a writing assignment.

00:10:10.827 --> 00:10:12.977
And just because you
log on, I could turn off

00:10:12.977 --> 00:10:14.957
and you could watch my
screen with my name,

00:10:14.957 --> 00:10:17.147
I might be taking a
nap, one of my siblings

00:10:17.147 --> 00:10:19.047
might be banging away
on the keyboard for me,

00:10:19.047 --> 00:10:20.707
or I might be distracted at home

00:10:20.707 --> 00:10:22.407
and dealing with other pressures.

00:10:22.407 --> 00:10:25.147
So what we can learn digitally,

00:10:25.147 --> 00:10:27.857
isn't always a signal and noise.

00:10:27.857 --> 00:10:29.777
There's a lot of noise out
there, we have to figure out

00:10:29.777 --> 00:10:32.577
where the signal is and
what's a value to us

00:10:32.577 --> 00:10:35.687
as we work alongside our
teachers to get them the tools

00:10:35.687 --> 00:10:38.537
they need and help them learn
how they can best use them.

00:10:39.867 --> 00:10:41.917
- And what does that look like right now?

00:10:42.757 --> 00:10:44.027
We're kind of a couple of weeks

00:10:44.027 --> 00:10:45.767
into this new school year now,

00:10:45.767 --> 00:10:49.457
how does digital learning
look in L.A. Unified?

00:10:49.457 --> 00:10:50.737
Is it pretty consistent?

00:10:50.737 --> 00:10:52.647
Are you seeing pockets of excellence,

00:10:52.647 --> 00:10:55.230
places where you think you
could see some improvement?

00:10:56.317 --> 00:11:00.127
- So we're 90, almost 99%
connected, kind of a start there.

00:11:00.127 --> 00:11:01.457
'Cause we started a new school year

00:11:01.457 --> 00:11:04.287
and so a high needs
population like we serve,

00:11:04.287 --> 00:11:06.217
there's a lot of transiency,
a lot of new families,

00:11:06.217 --> 00:11:07.320
families have moved.

00:11:08.227 --> 00:11:09.897
We did a survey of families,

00:11:09.897 --> 00:11:11.947
about a hundred thousand families,

00:11:11.947 --> 00:11:13.847
three quarters of the families we serve

00:11:13.847 --> 00:11:17.067
have had someone in the
household lose work due to COVID.

00:11:17.067 --> 00:11:19.837
So the impact on families is enormous.

00:11:19.837 --> 00:11:21.037
And so we started the school year

00:11:21.037 --> 00:11:23.597
with mostly about being connected.

00:11:23.597 --> 00:11:24.737
Are you safe and well?

00:11:24.737 --> 00:11:26.167
Do we know each other?

00:11:26.167 --> 00:11:27.397
'Cause that's the foundation for learning.

00:11:27.397 --> 00:11:28.827
If you don't have that right,

00:11:28.827 --> 00:11:30.507
you can't jump right into
the homework assignments,

00:11:30.507 --> 00:11:31.807
it's not gonna work that way.

00:11:31.807 --> 00:11:33.657
So we got to the 94% level.

00:11:33.657 --> 00:11:35.957
The next is we look at,
are they participating,

00:11:35.957 --> 00:11:38.057
students participating in
this type of engagement?

00:11:38.057 --> 00:11:40.087
Is there a dynamic
participation engagement

00:11:40.087 --> 00:11:42.050
between teachers and students?

00:11:43.167 --> 00:11:44.477
We're also at pretty high levels there.

00:11:44.477 --> 00:11:45.667
What's the daily attendance?

00:11:45.667 --> 00:11:47.167
There's a new state standard (indistinct),

00:11:47.167 --> 00:11:49.617
we comply with that and
how we measure attendance.

00:11:49.617 --> 00:11:51.387
I was just out of middle
school this morning,

00:11:51.387 --> 00:11:52.747
I said 99% attendance.

00:11:52.747 --> 00:11:55.517
Kids are attending, they're part of it.

00:11:55.517 --> 00:11:57.987
Now that engagement will
differ by community,

00:11:57.987 --> 00:11:59.907
will differ by type of student.

00:11:59.907 --> 00:12:02.237
We know that students who are struggling

00:12:02.237 --> 00:12:03.427
the most are our young learners.

00:12:03.427 --> 00:12:06.357
I haven't seen anyone yet show
me how you can teach someone

00:12:06.357 --> 00:12:08.850
to read over a Zoom call.

00:12:09.917 --> 00:12:11.027
Our English learners,

00:12:11.027 --> 00:12:12.957
those students with
differences or disabilities,

00:12:12.957 --> 00:12:14.007
those who might've been struggling

00:12:14.007 --> 00:12:16.197
before school services were closed.

00:12:16.197 --> 00:12:18.417
The challenge is greater now, for sure.

00:12:18.417 --> 00:12:20.267
The challenge is greater

00:12:20.267 --> 00:12:23.037
amongst our highest needs families.

00:12:23.037 --> 00:12:25.057
So if you look at our enrollment pattern

00:12:25.057 --> 00:12:26.167
compared to prior years,

00:12:26.167 --> 00:12:28.687
you'd see we're up in
fourth and fifth grade,

00:12:28.687 --> 00:12:30.597
we're up in high school,

00:12:30.597 --> 00:12:33.047
we're down meaningfully in kindergarten.

00:12:33.047 --> 00:12:36.184
So we had a bunch of educators
go into the community

00:12:36.184 --> 00:12:37.527
and try and better understand

00:12:37.527 --> 00:12:40.107
that and we heard back a couple of things.

00:12:40.107 --> 00:12:44.087
We heard that families
struggling to get by

00:12:44.087 --> 00:12:46.237
don't necessarily have
the support at home.

00:12:47.137 --> 00:12:49.487
We heard some families have given up hope,

00:12:49.487 --> 00:12:51.717
lost work and they've left,

00:12:51.717 --> 00:12:53.527
which is a relatively expensive community

00:12:53.527 --> 00:12:56.977
in which to live in
search of an opportunity.

00:12:56.977 --> 00:12:58.597
And we've learned that
some families have said,

00:12:58.597 --> 00:13:02.477
well, kindergarten, my
child's safe, they're in care,

00:13:02.477 --> 00:13:05.497
whatever care might be and until students

00:13:05.497 --> 00:13:08.207
are back in a school facility,

00:13:08.207 --> 00:13:10.197
the imperative is not there
to enroll in California,

00:13:10.197 --> 00:13:12.857
it's not law until the
child is six to be enrolled.

00:13:12.857 --> 00:13:14.887
So we've seen those challenges.

00:13:14.887 --> 00:13:17.077
So I could tell you kindergarten,

00:13:17.077 --> 00:13:19.597
engagement's tougher unless
you've got an adult with you,

00:13:19.597 --> 00:13:21.317
kids aren't logging on by themselves

00:13:21.317 --> 00:13:23.037
or participating by themselves.

00:13:23.037 --> 00:13:25.037
High schoolers, more capable
of learning independently.

00:13:25.037 --> 00:13:26.257
I was with a young man this morning,

00:13:26.257 --> 00:13:29.767
who's had perfect attendance
and is doing great

00:13:29.767 --> 00:13:33.877
and he's a water polo player,
he's got it all put together,

00:13:33.877 --> 00:13:35.457
but he's built that
foundation along the way.

00:13:35.457 --> 00:13:37.767
And so our young learners,
English learners,

00:13:37.767 --> 00:13:39.437
students with differences in disabilities,

00:13:39.437 --> 00:13:40.687
we need them back in the classrooms

00:13:40.687 --> 00:13:42.537
as soon as it's safe and appropriate.

00:13:43.607 --> 00:13:45.597
- And what does the classroom
experience look like?

00:13:45.597 --> 00:13:48.587
Is it a bit of whatever was happening

00:13:48.587 --> 00:13:50.917
in a physical environment
has not been transplanted

00:13:50.917 --> 00:13:53.507
to some combination of video conference

00:13:53.507 --> 00:13:56.737
and then assignments on the
Khan Academy's of the world,

00:13:56.737 --> 00:13:59.137
or is there a lot more variation?

00:13:59.137 --> 00:14:00.207
- There's more variation.

00:14:00.207 --> 00:14:03.117
I think, you know as well
not better than many,

00:14:03.117 --> 00:14:03.950
that would be a mistake.

00:14:03.950 --> 00:14:06.677
If we just tried to
replicate what we were doing,

00:14:06.677 --> 00:14:07.510
it wouldn't work.

00:14:07.510 --> 00:14:10.407
First of all, you'd have
eight hours Zoom days

00:14:10.407 --> 00:14:11.917
and both students and teachers

00:14:11.917 --> 00:14:13.077
would lose their minds from that.

00:14:13.077 --> 00:14:15.327
That's not a good or
effective way to teach.

00:14:15.327 --> 00:14:19.487
And so what we're seeing if,
the spring was that, you know,

00:14:19.487 --> 00:14:22.837
try to carry on, keep students engaged,

00:14:22.837 --> 00:14:25.637
but too late in the school
year to change much.

00:14:25.637 --> 00:14:27.127
Summer was learning for students

00:14:27.127 --> 00:14:29.177
and learning for our educators,
what can we do differently?

00:14:29.177 --> 00:14:31.437
How do we use the time differently?

00:14:31.437 --> 00:14:33.357
And the new school year
is more about that.

00:14:33.357 --> 00:14:35.767
So the engagement piece

00:14:35.767 --> 00:14:37.757
is focused on the interactive
and the technology

00:14:37.757 --> 00:14:41.487
where information might be found

00:14:41.487 --> 00:14:42.807
or a viewing of Khan Academy

00:14:42.807 --> 00:14:45.397
might be done separate from that.

00:14:45.397 --> 00:14:48.857
You find a little more
structure in the daily schedule,

00:14:48.857 --> 00:14:51.237
which can help families,
can help our aides

00:14:51.237 --> 00:14:54.577
and other non roster
classroom individuals,

00:14:54.577 --> 00:14:56.977
teachers to make sure
they can all participate.

00:14:56.977 --> 00:14:58.977
So a little more structured schedule.

00:14:58.977 --> 00:15:01.277
And we're just getting into assessments.

00:15:01.277 --> 00:15:03.357
We are assessing all students to see

00:15:03.357 --> 00:15:05.310
where they are with low stakes.

00:15:06.827 --> 00:15:08.447
Formative assessment of some sort,

00:15:08.447 --> 00:15:09.707
let's say DIBELS, for instance,

00:15:09.707 --> 00:15:13.107
in elementary for early readers.

00:15:13.107 --> 00:15:14.787
And that's a challenge, you know, DIBELS

00:15:14.787 --> 00:15:16.777
in a traditional first grade,

00:15:16.777 --> 00:15:19.167
you can probably get done in one day,

00:15:19.167 --> 00:15:23.147
assuming most of your students
are able to stay on task.

00:15:23.147 --> 00:15:26.176
You don't test for, I'm sorry,
you don't prepare for it

00:15:26.176 --> 00:15:27.977
as well as training for
it, so you want to see

00:15:27.977 --> 00:15:29.857
where they are and meet
them where they are.

00:15:29.857 --> 00:15:31.537
Now take DIBELS

00:15:31.537 --> 00:15:34.977
to a Zoom where on any given day

00:15:34.977 --> 00:15:37.847
one of your students may
struggle with the technology,

00:15:37.847 --> 00:15:41.617
they may be distracted
at home, may not be quiet

00:15:41.617 --> 00:15:43.437
or there might be
siblings or family members

00:15:43.437 --> 00:15:46.097
who inadvertently give
them help or get away.

00:15:46.097 --> 00:15:49.867
And the patience and
tolerance for young learners

00:15:49.867 --> 00:15:53.937
to go through all of that,
that's a tough journey.

00:15:53.937 --> 00:15:56.477
So we want that formative assessment

00:15:56.477 --> 00:15:57.917
because students need the feedback,

00:15:57.917 --> 00:16:00.417
their families need feedback
on where the work needs to be,

00:16:00.417 --> 00:16:01.457
how to build on the strengths

00:16:01.457 --> 00:16:03.710
and try to address some of the challenges.

00:16:04.667 --> 00:16:07.927
But it's not so simple to
say, a DIBELS in the classroom

00:16:07.927 --> 00:16:09.997
can go neatly and become a DIBELS online,

00:16:09.997 --> 00:16:11.047
it's not that simple.

00:16:12.467 --> 00:16:13.657
- Yeah and that's related to a question

00:16:13.657 --> 00:16:14.867
I have here from Facebook.

00:16:14.867 --> 00:16:16.887
Kids that care is part of their question.

00:16:16.887 --> 00:16:19.327
Some parents at my school are pushing back

00:16:19.327 --> 00:16:21.497
on the amount of screen
time and the youth,

00:16:21.497 --> 00:16:24.097
I guess in this case, Canvas
is a learning platform,

00:16:25.317 --> 00:16:27.347
have y'all done anything
to modify the curriculum?

00:16:27.347 --> 00:16:29.797
What learning platform,
the learning platform,

00:16:29.797 --> 00:16:32.130
maybe it's not as relevant,
but how are y'all,

00:16:32.977 --> 00:16:35.817
are there some guidelines
on the amount of screen time

00:16:35.817 --> 00:16:38.987
or is it still kind of learning
what families can handle

00:16:38.987 --> 00:16:42.699
because now the parents are
kind of the teaching assistants?

00:16:42.699 --> 00:16:45.549
- We sort of have, excuse me,
a minimum set of standards.

00:16:46.607 --> 00:16:48.100
But it's gonna be different.

00:16:49.207 --> 00:16:52.847
Without question, all of
this screen time limitations

00:16:52.847 --> 00:16:54.587
in particular young learners have,

00:16:54.587 --> 00:16:56.607
we have to be cognizant of that.

00:16:56.607 --> 00:16:57.440
They can't...

00:16:59.737 --> 00:17:00.570
Excuse me.

00:17:01.657 --> 00:17:03.620
So we have a set of standards.

00:17:04.947 --> 00:17:06.267
We leave flexibility

00:17:06.267 --> 00:17:08.467
in the hands of the
educator who knows best.

00:17:09.477 --> 00:17:12.117
Sal may wanna turn off
his camera, that's okay.

00:17:12.117 --> 00:17:14.467
If that's where Sal is, we
still want Sal to participate.

00:17:14.467 --> 00:17:16.987
He's still hearing, we
know he's participating,

00:17:16.987 --> 00:17:19.137
so we try not to be too rigid.

00:17:19.137 --> 00:17:20.717
And top down, we wanna hear

00:17:20.717 --> 00:17:23.237
from the voice of the
educator in the classroom.

00:17:23.237 --> 00:17:26.237
And you'll see, if you look at a schedule

00:17:26.237 --> 00:17:27.997
you'll see time on, time off.

00:17:27.997 --> 00:17:30.707
So the synchronous with
some asynchronous time

00:17:30.707 --> 00:17:31.627
and back to synchronous,

00:17:31.627 --> 00:17:33.737
as opposed to, we're
gonna have three hours

00:17:33.737 --> 00:17:34.970
straight of synchronous.

00:17:35.837 --> 00:17:37.367
You have an engaged audience I know,

00:17:37.367 --> 00:17:39.257
but after three hours listening to us,

00:17:39.257 --> 00:17:40.117
they would all be asleep

00:17:40.117 --> 00:17:41.727
or they'd have turned off their computers.

00:17:41.727 --> 00:17:44.687
And the same has gotta be recognized

00:17:44.687 --> 00:17:48.647
both from a student perspective
and an educator perspective.

00:17:48.647 --> 00:17:50.687
So yes, there has to be
boundaries around that.

00:17:50.687 --> 00:17:52.837
And I think sometimes people wanna look

00:17:52.837 --> 00:17:54.717
at the measure of synchronous time

00:17:54.717 --> 00:17:57.367
and say more is better, less is worse.

00:17:57.367 --> 00:17:59.447
Well, you're directionally right.

00:17:59.447 --> 00:18:01.717
But there's no science
as you and I both know

00:18:01.717 --> 00:18:04.137
on how much is too much
and so we're trying

00:18:04.137 --> 00:18:05.687
to go into that carefully and recognize

00:18:05.687 --> 00:18:06.657
the unique needs of students,

00:18:06.657 --> 00:18:09.767
because some will do better in this form,

00:18:09.767 --> 00:18:11.267
some will do better more independently.

00:18:11.267 --> 00:18:13.117
And we want teachers to
have that flexibility to do

00:18:13.117 --> 00:18:15.829
what they think is best
for their students.

00:18:15.829 --> 00:18:17.447
- And what about in terms of the number

00:18:17.447 --> 00:18:19.287
or the number of either subjects

00:18:19.287 --> 00:18:20.597
or the coverage of standards?

00:18:20.597 --> 00:18:21.917
You know, in a normal school year

00:18:21.917 --> 00:18:23.797
you're covering oftentimes six,

00:18:23.797 --> 00:18:25.277
in some cases, seven subjects

00:18:25.277 --> 00:18:28.197
if you include things like PE and art,

00:18:28.197 --> 00:18:30.517
and then obviously,
you're trying to cover,

00:18:30.517 --> 00:18:33.877
end standards in a year in
an English or math class.

00:18:33.877 --> 00:18:37.207
Is there a view to kind of free
up that a little bit to try

00:18:37.207 --> 00:18:39.197
to say, okay, this is
the really the must haves

00:18:39.197 --> 00:18:40.709
and then there's maybe some must to haves?

00:18:40.709 --> 00:18:42.447
- Yeah, you have to do that online

00:18:42.447 --> 00:18:45.027
and where that real task will come,

00:18:45.027 --> 00:18:47.357
no pun intended is hybrid.

00:18:47.357 --> 00:18:49.677
So I wanna talk about
hybrid and let's just unpack

00:18:49.677 --> 00:18:51.157
what that is or isn't.

00:18:51.157 --> 00:18:52.137
Hybrid exists because you can't get

00:18:52.137 --> 00:18:54.237
everyone back in the same classroom.

00:18:54.237 --> 00:18:56.517
It's not a choice anyone
will willingly make,

00:18:56.517 --> 00:18:58.497
you have online or everybody back.

00:18:58.497 --> 00:19:01.207
We all want everybody
back, but health protocols

00:19:01.207 --> 00:19:03.047
won't let anybody back at the same time.

00:19:03.047 --> 00:19:05.927
So you have to have smaller cohorts,

00:19:05.927 --> 00:19:07.517
which means some will be in a classroom

00:19:07.517 --> 00:19:10.477
and some will be somewhere
else accessed online.

00:19:10.477 --> 00:19:12.427
Okay, so that gets us to hybrid.

00:19:12.427 --> 00:19:15.407
Now think of the implication for

00:19:16.635 --> 00:19:18.987
a master schedule and secondary complex

00:19:18.987 --> 00:19:21.557
versus elementary.

00:19:21.557 --> 00:19:24.267
Elementary teacher could
be with some students

00:19:24.267 --> 00:19:26.197
in the morning and the
other half the students

00:19:26.197 --> 00:19:27.357
in the afternoon.

00:19:27.357 --> 00:19:29.707
And when the students
aren't with the teacher,

00:19:29.707 --> 00:19:31.717
there could be an aid,
a playground supervisor,

00:19:31.717 --> 00:19:33.887
there are other ways to
make sure that students

00:19:33.887 --> 00:19:36.397
are with us all day in elementary

00:19:36.397 --> 00:19:39.827
and the focus on the
core happens half day,

00:19:39.827 --> 00:19:40.927
that's manageable.

00:19:40.927 --> 00:19:43.497
And you can keep a small cohort together

00:19:43.497 --> 00:19:44.330
for medical reasons.

00:19:44.330 --> 00:19:47.767
Small meaning 12 students, teacher, aid,

00:19:47.767 --> 00:19:50.047
they'll fit within a classroom safely.

00:19:50.047 --> 00:19:52.307
If outbreak of a virus to occur,

00:19:52.307 --> 00:19:54.477
you can isolate it to a
relatively small group

00:19:54.477 --> 00:19:56.467
so it won't spread to the entire school,

00:19:56.467 --> 00:19:58.097
that's elementary.

00:19:58.097 --> 00:20:00.607
Let's go to the secondary
and more complex schedule,

00:20:00.607 --> 00:20:03.387
typical in our high schools
or secondary schools to six,

00:20:03.387 --> 00:20:05.457
six different classes.

00:20:05.457 --> 00:20:09.117
So 12 plus a teacher, an aid,

00:20:09.117 --> 00:20:13.577
go on to six, the math
will take you to two to 250

00:20:13.577 --> 00:20:16.249
individuals that one person
could come in contact

00:20:16.249 --> 00:20:18.277
with one or two degrees of separation

00:20:18.277 --> 00:20:19.657
because you go from here.

00:20:19.657 --> 00:20:21.957
You're not the same 12
all the way through.

00:20:21.957 --> 00:20:26.077
So when we go back to
schools in a real hybrid, not

00:20:29.033 --> 00:20:31.167
a call, let's say a medically sound hybrid

00:20:31.167 --> 00:20:33.347
where we're keeping the cohorts small,

00:20:33.347 --> 00:20:35.117
we're gonna have to look at
that master schedule and say,

00:20:35.117 --> 00:20:38.667
what's the priority of
in-person instruction?

00:20:38.667 --> 00:20:41.227
The rest may be at school instruction,

00:20:41.227 --> 00:20:43.527
but actually provided by
an educator who's online

00:20:43.527 --> 00:20:46.307
because we can't take you to sit

00:20:46.307 --> 00:20:49.467
all six of your classes in-person,

00:20:49.467 --> 00:20:50.987
because now we no longer
have a small cohort.

00:20:50.987 --> 00:20:52.827
You've been around 250 students,

00:20:52.827 --> 00:20:54.157
one of you or your teacher

00:20:54.157 --> 00:20:58.467
were to have the virus
occur, all 250 gone.

00:20:58.467 --> 00:21:01.167
That's not sound from health practices.

00:21:01.167 --> 00:21:04.227
So we're looking at that
now and as we transition

00:21:04.227 --> 00:21:06.497
at some point when it's safe
and appropriate in Los Angeles,

00:21:06.497 --> 00:21:09.187
it's not yet when it is,
we're gonna have to look

00:21:09.187 --> 00:21:12.397
at that complex master schedule
and to your original point,

00:21:12.397 --> 00:21:14.787
there's the got to haves
and the foundation pieces

00:21:14.787 --> 00:21:18.477
and then we will supplement
the others as best we can

00:21:19.527 --> 00:21:21.857
and not all of the requirements
to be able to be met

00:21:21.857 --> 00:21:23.887
in the same way we used to meet them,

00:21:23.887 --> 00:21:25.077
but we're in the middle of pandemic.

00:21:25.077 --> 00:21:30.077
And the point we look at and
say is, okay, online is online.

00:21:30.777 --> 00:21:32.957
For almost all students,

00:21:32.957 --> 00:21:36.117
being back in the classroom
is of benefit, okay?

00:21:36.117 --> 00:21:37.957
If we can only offer you online

00:21:37.957 --> 00:21:41.857
and in-person math and English,
that's better than online.

00:21:41.857 --> 00:21:43.437
It will pale in comparison

00:21:43.437 --> 00:21:44.927
to what we used to do six months ago,

00:21:44.927 --> 00:21:47.477
when you could have French and music

00:21:47.477 --> 00:21:49.167
and all of your electives

00:21:49.167 --> 00:21:51.117
and everything else in-person as well.

00:21:51.117 --> 00:21:52.947
But if we can't, we can't.

00:21:52.947 --> 00:21:55.997
And so the health boundary
and the instructional boundary

00:21:55.997 --> 00:21:59.410
have to coexist in the
same program for students.

00:22:00.857 --> 00:22:01.987
- Now, it makes a ton of sense.

00:22:01.987 --> 00:22:03.587
And I can only imagine, I mean,

00:22:03.587 --> 00:22:05.727
scheduling is hard during a normal year.

00:22:05.727 --> 00:22:08.947
I can't even imagine
that the contact tracing

00:22:08.947 --> 00:22:11.100
and the cohort size, et cetera, et cetera.

00:22:12.157 --> 00:22:13.877
I guess two questions in one,

00:22:13.877 --> 00:22:17.967
and this is related to one from Susan

00:22:17.967 --> 00:22:19.684
Garcia Dominguez.

00:22:19.684 --> 00:22:21.717
"Hello Superintendent Beutner,

00:22:21.717 --> 00:22:23.627
what rules need to be
rewritten for online learning?

00:22:23.627 --> 00:22:26.640
I'll expand that a
little bit for, you know,

00:22:28.361 --> 00:22:29.194
what has been the learning?

00:22:29.194 --> 00:22:31.857
I mean, you mentioned there's
been learnings this year,

00:22:31.857 --> 00:22:33.707
what do you think is the loss?

00:22:33.707 --> 00:22:35.137
What are kind of your fears here,

00:22:35.137 --> 00:22:37.117
that maybe the damage
that has already been done

00:22:37.117 --> 00:22:40.567
and that might occur over
the coming six months or so?

00:22:40.567 --> 00:22:43.027
And then what are silver
linings if anything

00:22:43.027 --> 00:22:45.867
of this maybe from some
of what the rule rewriting

00:22:45.867 --> 00:22:47.087
or whatever it might be??

00:22:47.087 --> 00:22:47.920
- Yeah.

00:22:47.920 --> 00:22:51.167
So I won't use the word rule
too much, but I think the loss

00:22:51.167 --> 00:22:52.677
and the potential benefits, I guess.

00:22:52.677 --> 00:22:55.397
The loss is a learning loss, for sure.

00:22:55.397 --> 00:22:57.517
We spoke about already.

00:22:57.517 --> 00:23:00.177
A lot of research on
what the summer slide is.

00:23:00.177 --> 00:23:02.337
Now students have been in online learning,

00:23:02.337 --> 00:23:04.377
but there's still been a slide for many.

00:23:04.377 --> 00:23:05.917
That's

00:23:05.917 --> 00:23:07.437
not hard to

00:23:07.437 --> 00:23:09.057
assume that's the case,
hasn't been studied,

00:23:09.057 --> 00:23:10.587
but let's assume that's the case.

00:23:10.587 --> 00:23:14.037
And as each week and month goes
by, we're already at the six

00:23:14.037 --> 00:23:15.737
month point where students
have been in classrooms.

00:23:15.737 --> 00:23:18.020
So there will have been
learning loss for many.

00:23:19.467 --> 00:23:23.547
There will be the social emotional side,

00:23:23.547 --> 00:23:25.007
which is not getting enough attention.

00:23:25.007 --> 00:23:27.340
It started in this country
as a health crisis,

00:23:28.437 --> 00:23:30.427
is becoming an education crisis,

00:23:30.427 --> 00:23:32.677
will become a mental health crisis

00:23:32.677 --> 00:23:34.977
and so many of the
challenges we face in society

00:23:34.977 --> 00:23:36.157
present themselves in schools.

00:23:36.157 --> 00:23:39.407
So we're expecting and
planning for when students

00:23:39.407 --> 00:23:43.267
are back in campuses that some
of the most foundational work

00:23:43.267 --> 00:23:46.147
will need to be done around
the social emotional society

00:23:46.147 --> 00:23:49.097
to allow educators to
move on and start to get

00:23:49.097 --> 00:23:50.257
into the learning loss

00:23:50.257 --> 00:23:53.207
and how we not only catch
up, but take students ahead.

00:23:53.207 --> 00:23:54.967
Now we're trying to
intervene along the way,

00:23:54.967 --> 00:23:57.507
we are doing one-on-one instruction

00:23:57.507 --> 00:23:59.257
where we can do it safely outside,

00:23:59.257 --> 00:24:01.647
we've set up online tutoring for free

00:24:01.647 --> 00:24:03.347
to students and their families.

00:24:03.347 --> 00:24:06.327
So we're trying to intervene where we can,

00:24:06.327 --> 00:24:08.717
but the loss in learning,

00:24:08.717 --> 00:24:12.497
the trauma and the challenge
when we go back to classrooms

00:24:12.497 --> 00:24:13.337
is gonna be enormous.

00:24:13.337 --> 00:24:15.597
I mentioned earlier, you
know, 75% of families

00:24:15.597 --> 00:24:17.267
that have had someone whose work,

00:24:17.267 --> 00:24:18.607
that's trauma in a household,

00:24:18.607 --> 00:24:22.147
which may have already
been struggling to get by.

00:24:22.147 --> 00:24:25.257
God forbid, a loss of life
or health and safety issue

00:24:25.257 --> 00:24:26.547
with someone in the family due to COVID.

00:24:26.547 --> 00:24:31.297
So that's coming, those
are all the deficits.

00:24:31.297 --> 00:24:34.117
Now the challenge in the use of technology

00:24:34.117 --> 00:24:38.698
can and I believe will
become of some benefit to us.

00:24:38.698 --> 00:24:43.698
It will not supplant to replace
great in classroom learning,

00:24:43.737 --> 00:24:47.007
but look at what we did with
Fender and say, it's an ant.

00:24:47.007 --> 00:24:49.047
The classes with Fender are teacher led.

00:24:49.047 --> 00:24:51.567
So our music teachers
are leading the classes.

00:24:51.567 --> 00:24:54.457
We have Fender bringing
in their secret sauce,

00:24:54.457 --> 00:24:55.697
bringing the Fender Play app

00:24:55.697 --> 00:24:58.137
on top for students who want more

00:24:58.137 --> 00:25:01.027
and one in one is about
five, it's amazing.

00:25:01.027 --> 00:25:03.787
The work we do with the Alicia
Keys and Russell Westbrook

00:25:03.787 --> 00:25:06.977
around Snapchat is a different way

00:25:06.977 --> 00:25:10.367
'cause if we think about
the challenge in online

00:25:10.367 --> 00:25:11.717
is also an opportunity.

00:25:11.717 --> 00:25:13.657
Challenge in online is
students aren't with us,

00:25:13.657 --> 00:25:15.417
they're not a captive audience.

00:25:15.417 --> 00:25:16.947
So we've got to keep them engaged.

00:25:16.947 --> 00:25:19.337
The opportunity is to
figure out how to do that.

00:25:19.337 --> 00:25:21.117
Use different tools and technologies,

00:25:21.117 --> 00:25:22.147
meet them where they are.

00:25:22.147 --> 00:25:24.317
Alicia Keys is meeting them
right where they are there.

00:25:24.317 --> 00:25:25.757
They're coming to her,

00:25:25.757 --> 00:25:28.847
they're actually not coming
to class to meet Alicia Keys.

00:25:28.847 --> 00:25:30.997
So if we can take some of
those skills and learnings

00:25:30.997 --> 00:25:33.377
and bring them back with
us to the classroom,

00:25:33.377 --> 00:25:35.347
you know, I hope the Alicia
Keys Snapchat book club

00:25:35.347 --> 00:25:36.837
is around with us for a long, long time

00:25:36.837 --> 00:25:38.387
as with the Fender music class

00:25:38.387 --> 00:25:39.917
and some of the things we're doing.

00:25:39.917 --> 00:25:42.567
So when we do get back to classrooms,

00:25:42.567 --> 00:25:46.377
we'll know a lot more
about how we can make

00:25:46.377 --> 00:25:49.437
the use of technology an important part

00:25:49.437 --> 00:25:52.067
of the everyday experience
for all students

00:25:52.067 --> 00:25:54.027
and we'll learn a lot more
about how we keep students

00:25:54.027 --> 00:25:55.207
engaged, not just online,

00:25:55.207 --> 00:25:57.007
but actually in a classroom setting.

00:25:58.615 --> 00:26:01.427
- And you know, hopefully
maybe this time, next year,

00:26:01.427 --> 00:26:04.967
things have close to
normal or back to normal,

00:26:04.967 --> 00:26:07.767
how are you gonna be
viewing your job then?

00:26:07.767 --> 00:26:09.947
Is it a lot of just, you know, well,

00:26:09.947 --> 00:26:12.607
I don't wanna color your
answer too much, but is it,

00:26:12.607 --> 00:26:14.367
there's the learning loss

00:26:14.367 --> 00:26:15.847
you'll have to deal
with, what other things?

00:26:15.847 --> 00:26:16.680
And there's the things

00:26:16.680 --> 00:26:19.117
that you don't wanna lose potentially.

00:26:19.117 --> 00:26:19.950
- Yeah.

00:26:21.117 --> 00:26:22.607
I don't wanna make this
a money conversation

00:26:22.607 --> 00:26:24.587
'cause I think it's more important
as a values conversation,

00:26:24.587 --> 00:26:26.637
what we have to do to make sure

00:26:26.637 --> 00:26:28.477
our students have that
opportunity to learn.

00:26:28.477 --> 00:26:31.007
And as you know so well
learning's cumulative.

00:26:31.007 --> 00:26:34.067
So those young learners who
aren't building the foundation

00:26:34.067 --> 00:26:36.737
skills and literacy in
math and critical thinking

00:26:36.737 --> 00:26:40.367
will have a lifetime of
consequences unless we get on that.

00:26:40.367 --> 00:26:42.997
But there's a money issue looming too.

00:26:42.997 --> 00:26:45.717
And for whatever set of reasons,

00:26:45.717 --> 00:26:49.037
schools are the forgotten child
as part of the conversation

00:26:49.037 --> 00:26:51.120
about how we recover from COVID.

00:26:52.077 --> 00:26:55.407
The nation as a whole has risen
to the occasion to respond

00:26:55.407 --> 00:26:56.567
to the needs of healthcare community.

00:26:56.567 --> 00:26:58.267
That's great, we should.

00:26:58.267 --> 00:27:00.757
No one should be without whatever
the appropriate treatment

00:27:00.757 --> 00:27:02.237
is and those clinicians

00:27:02.237 --> 00:27:04.187
on the front line should be supported.

00:27:04.187 --> 00:27:06.577
The front lines are gonna
be in our classrooms.

00:27:06.577 --> 00:27:08.697
And at the same time we are facing

00:27:08.697 --> 00:27:12.067
the need to invest more,
to provide adult meals,

00:27:12.067 --> 00:27:14.867
meals for adults because
they're hungry, we should.

00:27:14.867 --> 00:27:16.487
To provide devices, internet access,

00:27:16.487 --> 00:27:19.517
where we receive no
dedicated funding for that.

00:27:19.517 --> 00:27:22.567
The cost of testing and tracing programs,

00:27:22.567 --> 00:27:23.947
sanitizing and cleaning classrooms.

00:27:23.947 --> 00:27:25.827
We changed the air filtration system

00:27:25.827 --> 00:27:26.907
in every one of our schools,

00:27:26.907 --> 00:27:30.047
80 million square feet of
buildings, replaced the filters.

00:27:30.047 --> 00:27:32.517
We went from a MERV 8 to a MERV 13,

00:27:32.517 --> 00:27:35.069
we just skipped all the
other MERVs in between.

00:27:35.069 --> 00:27:37.207
And what a MERV 13 is, the filters,

00:27:37.207 --> 00:27:41.907
the same membrane as an N95
mask, all that costs money.

00:27:41.907 --> 00:27:45.537
And so as we're transitioning
from a healthcare crisis

00:27:45.537 --> 00:27:48.802
to an education crisis
and the social emotional

00:27:48.802 --> 00:27:50.777
(indistinct) of students
that come with that,

00:27:50.777 --> 00:27:54.797
if there's not recovery funding,

00:27:54.797 --> 00:27:56.237
schools are gonna be in a world of hurt.

00:27:56.237 --> 00:27:58.067
Because municipal budgets, state budgets,

00:27:58.067 --> 00:27:59.887
where most school funding in California,

00:27:59.887 --> 00:28:01.097
it all comes from the state,

00:28:01.097 --> 00:28:02.417
States are under a lot of budget pressure,

00:28:02.417 --> 00:28:04.717
they've already said constant looming.

00:28:04.717 --> 00:28:06.607
And if the federal government
or the state government

00:28:06.607 --> 00:28:10.190
can't step up, at the same
time, we have to invest more.

00:28:11.077 --> 00:28:12.537
We're looking at budget cuts

00:28:12.537 --> 00:28:15.130
and the intersection of those two things,

00:28:16.037 --> 00:28:16.987
it's not promising.

00:28:18.747 --> 00:28:19.580
- Yeah.

00:28:19.580 --> 00:28:22.987
Now, well, I wish there
was a rosier outlook,

00:28:22.987 --> 00:28:25.377
but, I mean, that sounds like a very real

00:28:25.377 --> 00:28:27.977
set of circumstances that
we're gonna have to struggle

00:28:27.977 --> 00:28:29.317
with over the coming year.

00:28:29.317 --> 00:28:30.357
Well, you know, these conversations

00:28:30.357 --> 00:28:32.817
always go a lot faster than I expect.

00:28:32.817 --> 00:28:35.577
And I know, I mean, we
just talked about it,

00:28:35.577 --> 00:28:36.927
all that you have on your plate

00:28:36.927 --> 00:28:38.837
and it's not an enviable position,

00:28:38.837 --> 00:28:41.057
but it gives me comfort that folks

00:28:41.057 --> 00:28:43.407
like yourself are leading the charge

00:28:43.407 --> 00:28:46.097
in how we navigate this
very tough situation.

00:28:46.097 --> 00:28:48.367
So thank you so much
Superintendent Beutner

00:28:48.367 --> 00:28:49.767
for joining us today.

00:28:49.767 --> 00:28:51.433
- Thank you. And thank you for having me

00:28:51.433 --> 00:28:52.507
and thank you and your colleagues.

00:28:52.507 --> 00:28:55.877
We found the work you're doing
at Khan Academy invaluable.

00:28:55.877 --> 00:29:00.307
It's become a real, not just
option, but real necessity

00:29:00.307 --> 00:29:02.347
for so many of our educators
to bring into the work.

00:29:02.347 --> 00:29:03.517
So thank you for the work you do

00:29:03.517 --> 00:29:05.667
and all your colleagues, I appreciate it.

00:29:05.667 --> 00:29:08.080
- No, well it's our honor to
be able to help. Thank you.

00:29:10.107 --> 00:29:13.417
Well, thank you everyone for
joining today's live stream,

00:29:13.417 --> 00:29:14.367
I thought it was...

00:29:15.847 --> 00:29:18.747
Being a superintendent is a
hard job in any circumstances,

00:29:18.747 --> 00:29:20.267
especially of a school district,

00:29:20.267 --> 00:29:23.977
the size of Los Angeles Unified
with 700,000 plus students.

00:29:23.977 --> 00:29:26.357
You could only imagine
how hard it is in a time

00:29:26.357 --> 00:29:28.167
like this when so much is changing.

00:29:28.167 --> 00:29:29.367
So it's really heartening.

00:29:29.367 --> 00:29:32.547
I know Superintendent Beutner
from before this conversation,

00:29:32.547 --> 00:29:34.287
and I've always been
impressed with his work,

00:29:34.287 --> 00:29:37.307
but not an easy situation to go in.

00:29:37.307 --> 00:29:38.707
But I thought it was really valuable,

00:29:38.707 --> 00:29:41.557
kind of understanding all of the levers.

00:29:41.557 --> 00:29:43.827
So with that, thanks for joining today.

00:29:43.827 --> 00:29:45.667
Some of you all might have
known through social media,

